## Related Papers

### Plan-on-Graph (PoG): Self-Correcting Adaptive Planning of Large Language Models on Knowledge Graphs

Authors:
Liyi Chen et al.

Year:
2024

arXiv:
https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.23875

PDF:
https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.23875.pdf

### Summary

Plan-on-Graph (PoG) proposes a planning framework that
combines knowledge graphs, memory, reflection, and
self-correcting reasoning.

Instead of generating actions directly, the system
decomposes complex goals into smaller planning steps and
adaptively navigates through structured knowledge.

### Key Concepts

- Knowledge Graph Navigation
- Goal Decomposition
- Multi-Step Planning
- Adaptive Reasoning
- Self-Correction

### Industrial AI Relevance

Industrial systems rarely solve problems in a single step.

Production scheduling, energy optimization, maintenance,
and supply chain coordination require sequences of
interdependent decisions.

PoG demonstrates how planning can emerge from structured
knowledge representations instead of isolated model outputs.

### Industrial AI Lens

- Planning is distinct from prediction
- Complex decisions require intermediate states
- Knowledge graphs provide navigable decision spaces
- Self-correction improves robustness

### Relation to Agentic Orchestration

PoG supports the idea that orchestration should be built
around explicit planning and coordination mechanisms
rather than direct action generation.

The framework is particularly relevant for orchestrating
multiple bounded decisions within larger decision graphs.

